




  I. About

    ~~~
                                                       ,,
    `7MM"""YMM                                         db
      MM    `7
      MM   d  .gP"Ya `7M'   `MF'`7MMpMMMb.`7M'   `MF'`7MM  `7MMpMMMb.pMMMb.
      MM""MM ,M'   Yb  VA   ,V    MM    MM  VA   ,V    MM    MM    MM    MM
      MM   Y 8M""""""   VA ,V     MM    MM   VA ,V     MM    MM    MM    MM
      MM     YM.    ,    VVV ,,   MM    MM    VVV      MM    MM    MM    MM
    .JMML.    `Mbmmd'    ,V  db .JMML  JMML.   W     .JMML..JMML  JMML  JMML.
                        ,V
                     OOb"
    ~~~

Fey.nvim is a plugin that aims to be for the Fey markup language what
nvim-orgmode/orgmode is for the org-mode markup language.

I use that particular comparison because just like org-mode, Fey has a tree-like
heading hierarchy, and the first features that have been implemented are related
to moving subtrees up/down or promoting/demoting them (or individual headings).

We started here because it's incredible how powerful taking notes feels even when
all you have as a super-power is organizing the sections/subsections of your
ideas. 95% of what we have found that we use org-mode for was just organizing
text content with headings.

If you're curious what Fey looks like in use, pay attention to this README.fey
as well as the README.fey of the Fey specification identified in section I.i.


  I.A. Special Note

I want to be clear that because of the similarity between Fey and Orgmode I've
based the Fey.nvim plugin off the nvim-orgmode/orgmode plugin as a starting
point. As time goes on, much of the code will be different to accomodate for the
specificities of the Fey markup grammar, but so much of what Fey can do already
is due to the aboslutely incredible architecture that nvim-orgmode/orgmode has
which has made developing Fey.nvim incrementally a much easier process than if I
had to begin from scratch.


  I.A.i. Plugin Documentation

This README is the current description of what works: section II. below. The
:help documentation is behind it (see `doc/fey_api.txt` for the Lua API, which
is up to date), as a rough pass of project-wide
find-and-replace was done to update all references to 'org' and 'orgmode' to
'fey'. This means that sometimes words like 'organization' are currently
'feyanaization' which is humorous but will be updated eventually.

Some features in the documentation are also not working or disabled. Check out
the repo file `/lua/fey/config/defaults.lua` to see what is more or less
supported as section II. below about the current feature set won't always be
present in this README.fey.


  I.B. Fey specification

The Fey markup language is defined currently by the README.fey of its github
repository. It is a work in progress as many syntax features have not yet been
decided.

For example, the URL syntax below is experimental and not final, its use here is
to experiment with how it looks and feels to use.

[url] https://github.com/Loreite/Fey [Fey Markup Language Specification]


  II. Current Feature Set

  II.A. Standout feature: Automatic Headings

If you read the Fey specification you will notice that the headings are defined
in a way that would make them difficult to maintain manually as the tree
hierarchy changes when headings are moved, added, or removed, etc.

Thus, the first truly important feature for this plugin to satisfy was the
automatic tracking and reindexing of heading enumerated depth tokens.

That is to say the 'II' in 'II.' is the depth token and the '.' is the depth
delimiter. Each depth maintains its own ordered count in the style of the first
token-type encountered at that depth for the whole page (it is configurable
to set it so that token-type per depth is per-subtree).

It is possible to have an 'anonymous' depth-token at any given depth which will
not contribute to the count. Eg. 'II..' has an anonymous depth-token at depth
two. This allows users to define their headings with markdown style hashes '###'
or fey-mode signature '***' such as below.

You can read the Fey specification for a full description of what characters are
available to be used as either a depth- token or delimiter.

  ### Markdown anonymous hash example
  *** Fey anonymous star example


  II.B. Standout feature: Automatic Lists

Lists follow a similar specification as headings, except that where a heading
signature can have multiple segments, eg 'II.A.', bullets can only have one
segment, but it can also be anonymous to create an unordered listitem.

The bullets of lists and their sublists act much like the different segments of
a heading, where each new depth of a sublist is akin to adding a new segment
onto a heading. The only real difference then between a heading and a list is
that lists are hierarchically defined by their indentation.

Unordered listitems can be intermixed with ordered listitems, which has the same
cumulative effect on the following listitems as anonymous segments in a heading
does, ie. it skips it in the ordered count.

for example, the following ordered list is compared to equivalent headings:

    I.  same as heading 'I.'
        A.  same as heading 'I.A.'
            i.  same as heading 'I.A.i.'
            ii.  same as heading 'I.A.ii.'

        .  anonymous heading does not affect ordering

        B.  same as heading 'I.B.'
            i.  same as heading 'I.B.i.'

    II.  same as heading 'II.'
        A.  same as heading 'II.A.'
        .  etc...


  II.C. Basic feature: Blocks for Raw Text

You already know how these work if you're familiar with markdown ``` fences or
org-mode's #+begin_/#+end_ blocks. Fey's approach looks more like markdown,
except that blocks have a wider range of characters that can be used for the
open and close fences. In fact its the same set of characters as for heading
delimiters.

Blocks can be indented to any level, and their content is only requried to be
indented at a minimum to the same level. Running the command to fix indentation
will do that automatically for you.

    ###
    this is some raw text
        this will maintain its indentation
            so will this
    ###


  II.D. nvim-orgmode/orgmode Working Feature

Because this plugin is based on another plguin, most of the config options are
left untouched, or may be turned off to keep the plugin from breaking on
startup. This has primarily been done by commenting out all of the default
mappings. See the section on Available Default Mappings for the list of what is
currently supported.

Otherwise, this list is about features that we have gotten working out of the
box with little additional effort, or as a side effect of work on other
features.


  II.D.i. Indent Mode

You can toggle virtual or hard heading-aligned indetation by running the
following command `:Fey indent_mode`. This indents the body content of a heady
to align with its title. This may or may not be desirious, especially since
certain types of heading indexes are variable in width, this not all 7th-level
headings will indent to the same column.

If you would like that behaviour, stick to using anonymous headings and the
indentation will behave exactly as nvim-orgmode/orgmode

Config options which affect this indentation:

  -  fey_adapt_indentation = false,
  -  fey_startup_indented = false,
  -  fey_indent_mode_turns_off_fey_adapt_indentation = true,
  -  fey_indent_mode_turns_on_hiding_signature = true,


  II.D.ii. Edit Special

Inside of a Block, you can use the keybind <leader>;' to edit the contents in a
separate temporary buffer, with the changes updated in the block once you quit
to close the buffer.

search `:h fey-fey_edit_special` for more information.



  II.E. Headings that know things: tags, status, labels, properties

Phase one of turning this plugin from an org-mode clone into a Fey one was to stop
borrowing org's way of writing metadata (stars, drawers, `:TAGS:`, `SCHEDULED:`
lines) and use what Fey already has: tags.

A tag is a small piece of structure inside the text: a name, plain values, and
key-value pairs. The scope form is written with the hash sigil and is used for
metadata, the reference form with the at sigil is used for things that point or
refer, such as links and dates.

    ###  src fey
      I. {# status, TODO, A #} Hand in the essay {# labels, school, csci/103 #}
    {# scheduled, 2026-10-08 Thu 18:00 #}
    {# deadline, 2026-10-09 Fri 23:59 #}
    {# prop; effort: 2h; category: Coursework #}

    Some text, and a date inside it {@ date, 2026-10-07 Wed 10:00-11:00 @}.
    ###

The metadata of a heading is the tags in its title line plus the tag-only lines
right under it. That region is what the plugin reads and what it edits in place,
so setting a priority or a label changes just that tag and leaves the rest of
your line alone.

  II.E.i. Status: the todo keyword and the priority

The status tag holds the todo keyword first and an optional priority second. The
keywords are the ones of `fey_todo_keywords`. Without a keyword the priority
goes in a key: a status tag with a priority key and no values.

    ###  src fey
      I. {# status, TODO #} A task
      II. {# status, DONE #} A finished task
      III. {# status, NEXT, B #} A task with a priority
      IV. {# status; priority: C #} A priority and no keyword
    ###

  -  `cit` and `ciT` change the keyword forward and backward
  -  `cir` and `ciR` lower and raise the priority, `<prefix>,` asks for one
  -  when the keyword moves to a done state a closed date is written, and moving it back removes it
  -  `fey_todo_keywords`, `fey_todo_keyword_faces`, `fey_priority_highest`,
     `fey_priority_default` and `fey_priority_lowest` work as they did

  II.E.ii. Labels

Org's tag lists are called labels in Fey: the word tag is reserved for the syntax.
Labels are written with a labels tag, in the title line, right under a heading,
anywhere in a section, or above the first heading for the whole file. They can be
nested with a slash, and searching for `design` finds `design/ui`.

A label in the title line stays out of the title: the title of the first heading
above is Hand in the essay. A heading counts its own labels, the labels of the
headings above it and the labels of the file, as org tags were inherited.

  -  `<prefix>sl` edits the labels of the heading under the cursor, with completion
     from the labels of the hollows you can see
  -  the index remembers what held each label (title, body, text, document or
     data), so a database can tell a label of a file from a label of a heading

The tag syntax around the status and the labels is concealed by default so a
heading reads as a heading: only the keyword and the label names show. Turn it
off with `fey_conceal_task_tags = false`, or toggle it with `<prefix>Tc`.

  II.E.iii. Properties

A prop tag turns its keys into properties of the heading. The agenda reads
`category` from it, the match language and the query language read any of them,
and `effort` is ready for the clock work that comes with phase two.

The names of all of these tags can be changed: `fey_status_tag_name`,
`fey_labels_tag_name`, `fey_property_tag_name`, `fey_date_tag_name` and the ones
for scheduled, deadline and closed.


  II.E.iv. The data of a document

What org kept in `#+` lines at the top of a file is the data of the document in Fey: the keys of a `table`
tag above the first heading (see the tutorial of the test vault for the whole data model). The plugin reads these
keys, any other key is yours and is just data the queries and databases can use:

    ###  src fey
    {# table; title: Project plan; category: Work; id: 7f3a #}
    {# table; todo: TODO NEXT | DONE CANCELLED; archive: archive/%s_old #}
    {# table; header_args: :tangle yes #}
    {# labels, project, plan/2026 #}
    ###

  -  `title` is the title of the file (else the file name) and `category` the category of its items in the agenda
  -  `todo` is a sequence of todo keywords with the done ones after a bar, `TODO NEXT | DONE`. Several
     sequences are a list in the body of a table tag, one bullet each. Without it the file uses `fey_todo_keywords`
  -  `archive` is where `<prefix>$` puts archived headings, a template like `fey_archive_location`
  -  `header_args` are the default header arguments of the source blocks of the file
  -  `id` is the identifier of the file, written when something links to it by id
  -  `labels` and the `labels` tag are the labels of the file. `aliases`, `author` and `email` are plain data
  -  completion offers these keys inside a `table` tag


  II.F. Dates and planning

A date is a date tag. The value is a timestamp with an optional weekday, time or
time range, repeater and warning delay. The same text works in the three planning
tags that live on the lines under a heading.

    ###  src fey
    {# scheduled, 2026-10-06 Tue 09:00-10:15 +1w #}
    {# deadline, 2026-10-09 Fri 23:59 -3d #}
    {# closed, 2026-10-02 Fri 21:10 #}
    {@ date, 2026-10-12 Mon--2026-10-13 Tue @}
    {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue; active: false @}
    ###

  -  a repeater is `+1w` (every week), `.+1d` (a day after it was done) or `++2m`
     (the next time in the future)
  -  a delay `-3d` on a deadline starts warning three days ahead, otherwise
     `fey_deadline_warning_days` decides
  -  a range is two dates joined by two dashes. An inactive date is for the record: it never shows in the
     agenda
  -  `<prefix>is` schedules and `<prefix>id` sets the deadline through a calendar popup that can also
     clear the date
  -  `<prefix>i.` inserts a date, `<prefix>i!` an inactive one, `cid` changes the date under the cursor
  -  `<C-a>` and `<C-x>` change the part of the date under the cursor (year, month, day, hour, minute),
     `<S-UP>` and `<S-DOWN>` move it a day, `<prefix>d!` toggles active and inactive
  -  one scheduled date, one deadline and one closed date per heading; the date tag can appear any number
     of times anywhere in the text


  II.F.i. Checkboxes, states and progress

A list item can start with a checkbox: `[ ]` open, `[x]` done, and any other
single mark that is not a digit or a bracket. A mark is a state: `[/]` in
progress, `[!]` important, `[n]` a note, `[?]` a question. The table below is
every state there is, with the class the rules look at.

    ###  src fey
      I. Shopping [1/2]

    -  [/] Groceries [1/2]
       -  [X] milk
       -  [ ] bread
    -  [!] Tools
    -  [n] The shop closes at six
    ###

  -  `<C-Space>` toggles done and open on the item under the cursor, and gives an item without a box one.
     `<prefix>sc` opens a list of the states to pick from
  -  every mark is one of five classes: open (still to do), active (under way), done, cancelled (closed
     without being done) and info (a note about the text, not a task)
  -  a parent follows its children: when everything that counts is done it is done, when nothing is done
     or under way it is open, otherwise it is in progress. Cancelled items and info items do not count
  -  a progress cookie, `[1/3]` or `[33%]`, at the end of an item or a heading counts the same way. A
     heading counts the items of its lists
  -  the vault indexes every box as a task of kind item (the mark is its state, only the class done is
     done), so TASK queries and the Lua API list them: `TASK WHERE kind = "item" AND !completed`
  -  an unknown mark is an open box

The box is shown as an icon of its state, in the colour of its class, and the
line the cursor is on shows it as written. `fey_show_checkbox_state_as_icons`
turns that off (or `b:fey_show_checkbox_state_as_icons` for one buffer),
`fey_checkbox_icons` is `nerd`, `unicode` or `auto`: Nerd Font glyphs when
nvim-web-devicons is installed, plain one cell symbols otherwise, and
`fey_checkbox_icon_overrides` gives single marks another icon.

