  II.F.ii. Footnotes

A footnote is a reference in the text and a definition, tied by a label. The
reference is a scope tag. The definition is any tag with the label that has a
body, so it can be written in the form you like: a pair tag, a line tag or a block
tag.

    ###  src fey
    The result is odd {@ fn, 1 @}, and so is this {@ fn, 2 @}, and this {@ fn, 3 @}.

    [ fn, 1 #]
    Measured twice.
    [# fn ]

    #[ fn, 2 ] Written on one line. #

    [ fn, 3 ]#
        A longer note, indented under the tag.
    ###

  -  `<prefix>nf` writes a reference at the cursor, with the next free number, and a new definition at
     the end of the file (under a heading called Footnotes when there is one), and puts you in its text.
     `fey_footnote_definition_form` (`pair`, `line` or `block`) is the form of the new definition
  -  `<prefix>o` on a reference jumps to the definition and offers to create it when it is missing; on a
     definition (its tag, or the opening line of a pair or block, or the closing line of a pair) it jumps
     back to the first reference
  -  the label is shown as superscript, and the syntax of the tags is hidden: `¹` for a reference, and the
     definition reads `¹ Measured twice.` The line of the cursor shows the tags as written.
     `fey_footnote_superscript` is `auto` (digits and signs, which every font has), `true` (letters
     too, which many fonts lack, so it is off by default) or `false`. A label that cannot be written whole
     stays as written. Neovim cannot ask a font what it can draw, so this is a switch, not a test
  -  the vault knows them: `FeyApiVault:footnotes()` lists every label with its references and
     whether it is defined, `{ missing = true }` finds the references with no definition, and
     `:checkhealth fey` reports them
  -  `fey_footnote_tag_name` renames the tag

  II.G. Hollows, vaults and the court

A hollow is a directory that contains a `.fey` folder. Its vault is the database in
that folder: the index of every Fey file below it (headings, tags, links, labels,
properties, dates, tasks), kept current while you edit, even before saving.
Hollows can sit inside hollows. The court is the top: one directory, by default
`~/feyhollow`, with a registry of every hollow and a merged view of all of them.

    ###  src lua
    require('fey').setup({
      fey_court_dir = '~/feyhollow',   -- the court, created when missing
      court = {
        enabled = true,
        agenda_dirname = 'agenda',     -- a directory of the court for notes that belong to no project
        refresh_on_start = true,       -- bring every vault up to date in the background
        jump_cwd = true,               -- jumping to a hollow changes the working directory
        jump_tab = false,              -- or opens it in a new tab
      },
    })
    ###

  -  `<prefix>vi` (or `:FeyHollowInit [name]`) makes the working directory a hollow and asks for its name.
     It registers with the nearest hollow above it, else with the court
  -  `<prefix>vr` brings the index up to date, `:FeyHollowsPrune` forgets hollows that are gone
  -  a hollow can opt out of merged views with `merge: false` in `.fey/hollow.fey`, `:FeyHollowMerge` flips it
  -  a hollow is named `court:notes:history` (the court, the hollow `notes`, its hollow `history`) and a file
     in it `court:notes:history/a.fey`. `current:sub/b.fey` is relative to the hollow you are in. Links, the
     query language, the capture targets and the refile picker understand these names

Views take a scope, which says which hollows they read: `current` (this hollow),
`tree` (it and the hollows below), `court` (all of them that did not opt out), or a
list of references, where `court:notes:*` means a hollow and everything below it.
The agenda, the query tag (`scope: court`), the database views (`gs`), refile and the
reminders all use scopes.


  II.G.i. Settings written in the notes

A note can carry the settings it wants to be edited with, and they are applied
while you edit, the way a modeline would be, but on every change and not only
when the file is loaded. Two tags do it: `nvim` for the editor and `plugin` for a plugin's options.

    ###  src fey
    {# nvim; colorscheme: tokyonight; tabstop: 4; wrap: false #}
    {# plugin, fey; fey_conceal_task_tags: false #}

    [ plugin, fey #]
    fey_checkbox_icons_:  nerd
    notifications_:
        reminder_time_:  5
    [# plugin ]
    ###

The first value of a `plugin` tag is the plugin's name, its keys are the options. For nested options use a
pair or a block tag: its body is read like the body of a `table` tag.

The same tags can be put where they apply to more than one note. The court's `.fey/config.fey`, and the
`.fey/config.fey` of each hollow, are read first, the widest first, and the note's own tags last:

  -  court (`~/feyhollow/.fey/config.fey`), then the hollows above the note, then the hollow it is in, then the note
  -  later wins, key by key. They are merged into one table before anything is applied, and only what differs from
     what is applied now is touched, so a court wide colour scheme holds until a hollow sets its own, and
     the hollow's holds until the note sets one. Take a tag away and the next layer's value comes back
  -  a config file that is open in a buffer counts with its unsaved changes
  -  `:FeySettings` shows the layers, where each setting came from and what was ignored, and why

When settings are applied is `settings.apply_on`: `load`, `enter` (the note becomes the current one), `change`
(after typing, `settings.debounce_ms`) and `save`; the default is the first three. `<prefix>?a` applies them
now, `<prefix>?t` applies the tag under the cursor, `<prefix>?e` switches applying on enter on or off.

What a note may set is limited, because a note can come from anywhere:

  -  `nvim`: only options that are local to the buffer or the window, plus `colorscheme` and `background`.
     Everything else, a global option or something that is not an option, is ignored. The names of options that
     run what is in them are denied until you allow them by name in `settings.allow`: expressions
     (`indentexpr`, `foldexpr`, `formatexpr`, `includeexpr`, `foldtext`, `statusline`, `winbar`, ...),
     functions (`omnifunc`, `completefunc`, `tagfunc`, ...), programs (`formatprg`, `equalprg`, `keywordprg`,
     `makeprg`, `grepprg`, `shell`, ...) and the editor's own safety switches. A colour scheme must be a plain name
  -  `plugin, fey`: only the options in a list of appearance and behaviour (the agenda, refile, footnotes,
     checkboxes, reminders). Where things live (the court, the vault), the mappings and anything that runs code
     are not in it. `settings.hot_options` adds names
  -  `plugin, <another plugin>`: only plugins you registered, in `settings.plugins` or with
     `require('fey.settings').register(name, { apply = fn, restore = fn })`. The tag only supplies data for your
     function; nothing is loaded by a name from a note
  -  `buf_enter` and `buf_leave` run Ex commands when a note is entered and left. **That is running code from the
     note.** They exist only when you name their keys in `settings.hooks` (`{ buf_enter = 'buf_enter' }`), which is
     off by default. Never turn it on for notes from places you do not trust: opening such a note would run
     its commands as you. `<prefix>!a`, `<prefix>!t` and `<prefix>!e` run them now, run the one under the cursor
     and switch running them on enter

Being careful with files from unknown sources is also about the court and hollow config files: whoever can
write `.fey/config.fey` changes the settings of every note under it.


  II.H. The navigator

The navigator is the Yazi style picker. `<prefix>;` opens it on the current file,
`<prefix>:` where you left it. It starts on the contents of the file (headings,
lists, tables, blocks and tags), and `h` keeps going up: out of the document into its
directory, up the directories to the root of the hollow, on through the hollows above
it, and finally to the court. `l` goes back down. A thin pane on the left shows the
level above so you can see where you are.

  -  `j`, `k` move, `l` or `<Tab>` goes in (`<Tab>` also enters the directory of a hollow), `h` goes up
  -  `<CR>` jumps to the object, file or hollow under the cursor, `<C-t>` does it in a new tab
  -  `H` switches to a picker of hollows only, `<prefix>vj` and `<prefix>vJ` open it directly
  -  `/` filters, `<C-u>` and `<C-d>` scroll the preview, `q` or `<Esc>` closes
  -  the preview is the file, the directory or the hollow, with syntax highlighting


  II.I. The agenda

`<prefix>a` opens the agenda prompt: `a` the week (or day), `t` all open tasks, `m` a match
query, `M` a match query of open tasks only and `s` a text search. The agenda
reads the vaults, so it needs no list of files, and it shows the hollows of
`fey_agenda_scope` (`court` by default). A project keeps its own agenda in its own
hollow and it shows up in the global one.

  -  items come from the scheduled and deadline tags, the date tags and ranges, and repeaters; overdue
     scheduled items stay on the agenda and deadlines warn ahead of time
  -  a line under the title says where the agenda reads from, and when more than one hollow is read each
     item gets a dimmed line with its hollow and file (`fey_agenda_show_scope`, `fey_agenda_show_hollow`)
  -  the match language is the one of org: `work-home` (labels), `priority="A"`, `deadline<"<today>"`, `effort>2`
     for properties, `/TODO|NEXT` for keywords, `|` for or. A label with a dash is written in quotes,
     `"csci-210"/TODO`
  -  `fey_agenda_files` is no longer used by the agenda. Use a scope; the agenda files of a custom command
     still limit that command
  -  archived headings stay out (`fey_agenda_skip_archived`)

Everything you do to an item is done to the heading in its file, through the same
code as the mappings in a document, and the view is rebuilt from the index:
`t` todo state, `<prefix>,` `+` `-` priority, `<prefix>t` labels,
`<prefix>id` and `<prefix>is` dates, `<prefix>r` refile, `<prefix>$` archive,
`<prefix>A` toggle the archive label. `<CR>` opens the item here, `<Tab>` in another window, `K` previews,
`f` and `b` move by a span, `.` is today, `vd` `vw` `vm` `vy` change the view, `J` jumps to a date, `/`
filters, `q` closes and `g?` lists the keys. If the file of the item has unsaved changes the
change is made in the buffer and left for you to save.


  II.J. Refile and archive

Refile moves a heading and its subtree under another heading, or to the end of
any file of the hollows in `fey_refile_scope`. `<prefix>r`, in a document or in the
agenda, asks for the destination with a fuzzy picker over every file and every
heading, written like `court:notes/a.fey  I.A. Title`.

The levels of the moved headings are adapted and the signatures are renumbered
in the destination and in the source. The destination is written before the
source is touched. `fey_refile_leave_link = true` leaves a link to the new place.
Links that pointed at the old place are counted and reported, not rewritten (yet).

Archive, `<prefix>$`, is a refile to the archive file of the document: `a.fey` goes
to `a.fey_archive` (`fey_archive_location` is a template). The heading is put at the top level of the
archive file with props that say where it came from: `archived_from`,
`archived_path`, `archived_at` and `archived_state`.


  II.K. Capture

`<prefix>c` opens a menu of capture templates. A template is Fey text with
expansions, written in a small window; `<C-c>` writes it, `<prefix>r` writes it to
a place you pick, `<prefix>k` throws it away.

    ###  src lua
    require('fey').setup({
      fey_capture_templates = {
        t = {
          description = 'Task',
          template = '  I. {# status, TODO #} %?\n{# scheduled, %t #}',
          target = 'court:notes/inbox.fey',
          heading = 'Inbox',                 -- a title or a signature like I.A.
        },
        j = {
          description = 'Journal',
          template = '  I. %<%H:%M> %?',
          target = '~/journal/journal.fey',
          datetree = true,                   -- year, month and day headings, in date order
        },
      },
    })
    ###

  -  `%?` is where the cursor goes, `%t` and `%T` are today and now written for a tag, `%u` an inactive date
     tag, `%a` a link to where you were, `%^{prompt|default}` asks, `%<format>` is `os.date`
  -  a target is a path or a name like `court:notes/inbox.fey`; the default is `fey_default_notes_file`,
     else `agenda/inbox.fey` in the court
  -  where in the file: `heading`, `datetree`, `regexp` or `query`, or the end
  -  `unique = true` refuses a title that already exists


  II.L. Notifications

With `notifications = { enabled = true }` a popup appears when a scheduled item or a
deadline is due (`reminder_time` minutes ahead, 10 by default; repeaters and
deadline warnings have their own times). The reminders come from the index, for
the hollows of `notifications.scope`. `require('fey').cron()` runs the same thing
headless for a cron job, with a system notification.


  II.M. The clock

A clock is a tag in the logbook of a heading, a pair tag in its own text. Clocking
in adds one, with no end; clocking out writes the end and the time it ran. The
vault indexes them as dates of the kind clock, so the running clock is found in
any file of any hollow and the time spent is a query.

    ###  src fey
      I. {# status, TODO #} Write the report
    [ logbook #]
    {# clock, 2026-10-06 Tue 13:00 #}
    {# clock, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00; end: 2026-10-06 Tue 11:30; dur: 1:30 #}
    [# logbook ]
    ###

  -  `<prefix>xi` clocks in on the heading under the cursor, stopping the clock that runs; `<prefix>xo` clocks
     out, `<prefix>xq` throws the running clock away, `<prefix>xj` jumps to the clocked heading and
     `<prefix>xe` sets the effort (the prop `effort`, like `2h`)
  -  in the agenda `I` `O` `X` do the same on the item, `<prefix>xj` jumps, `R` shows a clock report for
     the span and the clocked item is highlighted
  -  marking a task done clocks it out
  -  `fey.statusline()` gives `(Fey) [1:20/2h] (Write the report)` while a clock runs
  -  quitting with a clock running says so; it keeps running in the file
  -  `fey_clock_tag_name` and `fey_logbook_tag_name` rename the tags


  II.M.i. Notes and drawers

A drawer is a pair tag whose name is the drawer, in the text of a heading or of the
document. The logbook is one. A note is a list item with an inactive date tag, so
the vault indexes its date and a query finds it.

    ###  src fey
      I. {# status, DONE #} Write the report
    [ logbook #]
    -  {@ date, 2026-10-07 Wed 09:30; active: false @}  Closing note: sent it
    -  {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue 16:00; active: false @}  Note taken: waiting
       for the numbers
    [# logbook ]
    ###

  -  `<prefix>na` (in the agenda too) asks for a note and writes it on the heading under the cursor, the
     newest first in the drawer
  -  with `fey_log_into_logbook = true` the notes go into the logbook, else they are added at the end of the heading
  -  closing a todo and a repeating one write `Closing note:` and `State "NEW" from "OLD"` the same way
  -  the drawer is created when it is missing, under the metadata of the heading, in the form of
     `fey_drawer_form` (`'pair'` or `'block'`)
  -  a drawer may be a block tag too: `[ logbook ]#` and the lines indented under it. Both forms are found,
     and the clock and the notes work in either

    ###  src fey
      I. {# status, DONE #} Write the report
    [ logbook ]#
       {# clock, 2026-10-06 Tue 10:00; end: 2026-10-06 Tue 11:30; dur: 1:30 #}
       -  {@ date, 2026-10-06 Tue 16:00; active: false @}  Note taken: waiting
    ###


  II.M.ii. Comments

The tag `comment` (`fey_comment_tag_name`) comments its body in every form. The body is shown in the
comment colour (`FeyComment`, linked to `Comment`) and nothing in it is indexed: no tags, labels, links, dates or
checkbox items. The comment tag itself is still indexed. An optional boolean, as the first value or the key
`index`, turns that around for one comment: `{# comment, true #}` keeps indexing, `{# comment, false #}` ignores.
Without it the option `fey_comment_index_default` decides (default `false`, ignore).

    ###  src fey
    #[ comment ] a line #

    [ comment ]#
       a block, indented
    [ comment #]
    a pair
    [# comment ]
    ###

A scope tag comments its parent, the same node the `hl` tag colours:

  -  in a paragraph, title or table cell: that paragraph, title or cell
  -  alone on a list item with other contents (a paragraph, a table, a nested list): the item with all it holds
  -  the only content of a list item: the whole list it is in, the only way to comment a list with a scope tag
  -  at the top of a file, above the first heading: the whole file, so there is nothing to export
  -  in a section, not above a heading: the text of that section, not its subsections

`commentstring` is the line form, so `gc` writes `#[ comment ] text #`.


  II.M.iii. The clock table

A `clocktable` tag is a dynamic block: it runs like a query tag (`<prefix>qq` at the cursor, `<prefix>qa` for
the buffer, and when the buffer loads) and writes the time clocked into a `clocktable_result` pair tag after it,
replacing the last result. It reads the clocks of the index, so it needs no logbook parsing.

    ###  src fey
    {# clocktable; span: thisweek; by: heading #}
    [ clocktable_result #]
    | Heading                       | Time |
    +==============================+======+
    | {@ link, a.fey; section: I. @} | 1:30 |
    | Total                        | 1:30 |
    [# clocktable_result ]
    ###

  -  `span` (or the first plain value): `today`, `yesterday`, `thisweek` (the default), `lastweek`, `thismonth`,
     `lastmonth`, `thisyear`, `lastyear`, `all`, `7d` (the last 7 days), `2026`, `2026-10`, `2026-10-07`, or a range
     of two, `2026-10-01--2026-10-07`
  -  `by`: `heading` (the default, a link to each heading), `file` or `day`
  -  `scope`: as for a query tag, `current`, `tree`, `court` or hollows
  -  a finished clock counts when it starts or ends in the span; a running clock does not
  -  `fey_clocktable_tag_name` and `fey_clocktable_result_tag_name` rename the tags
  -  `fey_query_conceal_default = true` (it can be set from a note with the plugin tag) conceals all of them; `conceal: false` on a
     tag keeps that one in view
  -  `conceal: true`, on a `clocktable`, `query` or `feydb` tag, makes the tag and its result one object. Away from it you
     see the result and an icon where the tag was (a database or a clock, a one cell symbol without a Nerd Font;
     `fey_conceal_icons` changes it by tag name). The tag, its body and the head and closer of the result are hidden.
     With the cursor anywhere from the first line of the tag to the last line of the result, all of it shows as
     written. The tag writes its result as `[ clocktable_result; conceal: true #]`. Needs `conceallevel`


  II.M.iv. Math

The text of a `math` tag (`fey_math_tag_name`) is LaTeX and is highlighted as such, with the `latex` tree-sitter
parser when it is installed (without it nothing changes). A line tag is inline math, a block or a pair tag is
a display equation, and so is a fenced block named `latex`:

    ###  src fey
    The mass #[ math ] E = mc^2 # is energy.

    [ math ]#
       \int_0^1 x \, dx = \frac{1}{2}
    ###

The scope tag form is not injected: the head of a tag treats a backslash as an escape.


  II.M.v. Source blocks, edit special and tangling

A fenced block `###  src lua :tangle out.lua` has a language (the first parameter) and header arguments
(`:tangle`, `:noweb`, `:name`, `:mkdirp`, ...). The header arguments of a block are those of the document
(the data key `header_args`) under those of the headings (the prop `header_args`, outermost first) under its own.

  -  `<prefix>'` edits the block under the cursor in a window of its own, with the file type of the language,
     also in a list item (the indent of the fence stays) and in a tag; `<prefix>w` writes it back, `<prefix>'` writes
     and closes, `<prefix>k` throws the changes away, `g?` lists the keys
  -  `:tangle yes` writes the block to the file with the extension of its language (`fey_babel_extensions` adds
     some), `:tangle path` to that path, relative to the file; blocks with one target are written in order, blank
     line between
  -  `:noweb yes` (or `tangle`) replaces a line that is only `<<name>>` by the blocks of the same file with
     `:name name` (or `:noweb-ref`), with the indent of the line
  -  `<prefix>yt` tangles the file, `<prefix>yv` every file of the hollow, `<prefix>yc` finds what would go wrong and
     lists it in quickfix: a reference that names no block, blocks that refer to each other, a target that two files
     write (it is not written)
  -  the index has every block (language, name, target, arguments, content): `FeyVault:blocks`, and `fey.hollow.scope.blocks`


  II.M.vi. Links

Links are tags: `{@ link, target; desc: Title; section: I.A. @}` and `{@ section, I.A., file, 2 @}`. A link goes
to a file, a heading of a file, a url, a heading or file by its id, a file of another hollow
(`court:notes/a.fey`), or a scheme of your own.

  -  `<prefix>o` follows the link under the cursor, or the first link after it on the line; a bare url in text is
     opened too
  -  `<prefix>ls` stores the heading under the cursor (also in the agenda); `<prefix>li` picks a stored heading, or a file
     of the hollow and then one of its headings, and writes the link at the cursor, over the link the cursor is on, or over
     the visual selection, which becomes the description. The files and headings come from the index
  -  `fey_id_link_to_fey_use_id = true`: a stored heading gets the prop `id` and the link is `{@ link, id:..; desc: Title @}`,
     which still works when the heading moves or is renamed; a file has the data key `id`
  -  `<prefix>lc` puts the links that lead nowhere in quickfix: a file that does not exist, a heading it does not have, an
     id nothing has (`FeyVault:broken_links`, a query on the index)
  -  `fey_link_schemes = { jira = function(target, tag) ... end }` makes `{@ link, jira:ABC-1 @}` call your function
  -  in the agenda `<prefix>o`... follows the first link of the item; references of a heading in an editor (LSP) are its
     backlinks from the index
  -  a link can be a line, block or pair tag too, and then the text it holds is the link: `#[ link, a.fey ] some words #`, or
     a block or pair tag with a paragraph, a list or a table under it. Following climbs from the cursor to the nearest link
     tag, so a link inside the body of another link is the one that opens when the cursor is on it, and the text beside it
     opens the outer one
  -  `conceal: true` on a link or section tag, or `fey_link_conceal_default = true` (it can be set from a note with the plugin
     tag; `conceal: false` keeps one in view), hides its head: a scope tag is replaced by its `desc` (else the name of the file,
     with the section, or the url), a line, block or pair tag loses its head and closer and keeps its body. It comes back on the
     line of the cursor, and only there (a concealed query comes back with the cursor anywhere in it). `FeyLinkText` is the
     highlight of a replaced tag; needs `conceallevel`
  -  the old org style links (`[[file:..][..]]`, `Fey.links`, `hyperlinks.sources`) are gone


  II.M.vii. Completion

Inside the head of a tag the completion knows where it is (`omnifunc`, blink, nvim-cmp and LSP clients get the same
answers): the name after the opener, a value after a `,`, a key after a `;` and the value of a key.

  -  names: the tags of the plugin and those written in the notes of the hollow, most used first
  -  values: todo keywords and then priorities for `status`; labels for `labels`; files for `link`; the signatures of the
     file for `section`; footnote labels for `fn`; spans for `clocktable`; `fey` and the plugins you registered for `plugin`
  -  keys, with their colon: those a tag has (`desc`, `section`, `scope`, `conceal`, `by`, ...), the ones the notes already
     use with it, the keys of the document data inside `table`, Vim options inside `nvim` and the options a note may
     set inside `plugin, fey`
  -  values of a key: `section` the headings of the file, `scope`, `by`, `span` and the booleans, the colour schemes for
     `colorscheme`
  -  it works from the text before the cursor, so it also works while the tag is not finished and the file does not parse


  II.M.viii. Highlighting

  -  the head of every tag is coloured by its form (scope, line, block, pair), its values and keys by their own faces, and the body of
     a line, block or pair tag takes the colour of its tag
  -  the text of a date tag takes the face of its kind: `@fey.date`, `@fey.date.scheduled`, `@fey.date.deadline`, `@fey.date.closed`
  -  the deadline and the scheduled date of an open task that is past (the heading has a todo keyword that is not done, today is after the
     date) are painted `@fey.date.overdue` and `@fey.date.scheduled_past` from the index, again whenever the file is indexed, on entering a buffer
     and on focus; `fey_highlight_overdue = false` turns it off (a note may set it)
  -  the keywords and the labels of a heading are shown by `fey_conceal_task_tags` (II.E), checkboxes as icons (III.B), footnotes (III.C),
     concealed queries and links (III.G, III.K), all drawn by extmarks, the cursor line showing the text as written
  -  a closed fold shows `fey_ellipsis` in the colour of the text it hides


  II.M.ix. Heading commands

  -  `toggle heading` turns the heading under the cursor into plain text (its title), a list item or a plain line into a
     heading under the heading above it (a checked box becomes a done status tag, an open one a todo), at the next
     signature of that level; the headings below are renumbered. A count is the level of the new heading (`3` makes a
     third level heading) and is ignored when a heading becomes text
  -  `move subtree up` and `down` swap a heading, with all that is under it, with its neighbour of the same level, also from
     inside a closed fold; list items move inside their list
  -  new headings: after the content, as a sibling or as a subheading, with a todo status tag when asked
  -  promote and demote, with or without the subtree, renumber the signatures
  -  `next` and `previous visible heading` skip what a fold hides; same level, up the outline and last child jump over the tree
  -  `cycle` folds, opens and shows the children of the heading under the cursor, `global cycle` goes through contents, show all and
     overview; `fey_startup_folded` is `'overview'`, `'content'`, `'showeverything'` or `'inherit'`


  II.M.x. Export

`<prefix>e` opens the export menu. Markdown and HTML are written by Lua from the tree, with nothing to install; LaTeX, PDF, Word, OpenDocument and
EPUB are pandoc reading that Markdown (so pandoc is only needed for them); the iCalendar file comes from the dates in the index.
The file goes next to the note and the menu offers to open it.

  -  what is in it: sections as headings of the same level, the todo keyword before the title and the labels under it, paragraphs with the
     emphasis markers as emphasis and `` `code` ``, lists with their checkboxes, tables (the first row is the header), source blocks with their
     language, `math` as math (inline for a line tag, display for a block or a pair tag), footnotes at the end, links to other notes pointing at the
     exported file (`a.fey` becomes `a.md`, a section is an anchor `#sec-II-A`), the title and the author of the document data as the title page
  -  what is left out: everything a `comment` tag comments (a file that is all commented has nothing to export), the machinery of the notes
     (`prop`, planning dates, clocks, the logbook, the document data) and the tags that run queries; the result of a query is content, its links are links
  -  a table with merged cells is an HTML table with `colspan` and `rowspan` (embedded in the Markdown, the table itself in HTML); tags named like an
     HTML element (`span`, `mark`, `kbd`, `sub`, `sup`, `div`, `details`, `summary`, `blockquote`, ...) in the line, block or pair form are that element, with
     their keys as attributes, embedded in the Markdown or written as the element in HTML (event attributes and `javascript:` values are dropped, and the names
     of the tags of the notes keep their meaning: `section` is a link)
  -  names that conflict: a tag name that something else has (the plugin, an export of your own, a handler of the editor) keeps that meaning,
     and the weaker name ends with an underscore. `section_` is the HTML `section` where `section` is the link to a heading, `table_` is the HTML
     `table` where `table` is the data of the document; with a `div` tag of your own, `div_` is the HTML `div`. A name nothing else uses
     (`span`, `mark`, `kbd`, `details`) needs no underscore. Name your own tags with this in mind: a name ending with `_` is always read as the
     element without it
  -  a tag of your own gets its export from `tag_exports` in the setup (or `require('fey.export').tag(name, spec)`): a pattern for each form of the
     tag, where `%s` is what the tag holds, `%%` a percent sign and `\n` a new line

         ###  src lua
         require('fey').setup({
           tag_exports = {
             note = {
               block_tag = [[<aside class="note">\n%s\n</aside>]],
               pair_tag = [[<aside class="note">\n%s\n</aside>]],
               line_tag = [[<span class="note">%s</span>]],
               scope_tag = [[<div class="note">\n%s\n</div>]],
             },
           },
         })
         ###

     the HTML target puts the HTML of the body in the pattern; the Markdown target puts the Markdown of the body in it, with blank lines around a
     block so that it stays Markdown inside the HTML (GitHub and pandoc read it so). A scope tag wraps what it applies to (`Tag.body`): the paragraph, the
     list when it is alone in a list item, the item, the text of the section, the whole file above the first heading. A form can be a function
     `function(body, tag, target)` returning the text, which gets `tag.name`, `tag.form`, `tag.values` and `tag.keys` and can write anything for
     `'markdown'` or `'html'`; a table with the keys `html` and `markdown` gives each target its own forms (Markdown uses the HTML ones when it has none),
     and a form named `default` stands for the forms that are not there
  -  OpenDocument (`odt`) goes through pandoc next to Word
  -  `require('fey.export').markdown(text)`, `.html(text)` and `.ics(path)` give the text, `.file(format)` writes the file, `.label('topic', 'markdown')`
     exports the notes with a label
  -  `fey_custom_exports` adds entries to the menu; the emacs exporters and `emacs_config` are gone


  II.M.xi. Options, toggles and checks

  -  `setup` checks the options it is given and says what is wrong (nothing is refused): a name that is not an option (a typo), an option that is
     gone and what replaces it, a boolean or a tag name of another type, a value that is not one of the choices (`fey_drawer_form`,
     `fey_checkbox_icons`, `fey_startup_folded`, `fey_footnote_definition_form`)
  -  gone: `fey_use_cwd_config` and `fey_use_buffer_config` (configuration comes from the court, the hollows and the note), `fey_tags_column`,
     `fey_agenda_text_search_extra_files`, `emacs_config`, `hyperlinks`
  -  `:checkhealth fey` also reports the options of `setup`, and for every hollow: the state of the index and its schema (an old one is rebuilt at the next
     scan), the files that changed, appeared or went since they were indexed, the files that did not parse cleanly, and the tags nothing in the setup knows
  -  the toggles are under `<prefix>T`: `Tc` task tags, `Tl` links, `Tq` queries, `Ti` checkbox icons, `To` overdue dates, `Te` running the commands of a note when it is
     entered, `Ts` applying its settings (`<prefix>!e` and `<prefix>?e` still do the last two); each is the option of the same name switched until you set it again
  -  the heading commands that were waiting for their feature are on: `<prefix>*` toggle heading, `<prefix>iT` and `<prefix>it` insert a todo heading, `g?` the help


  II.M.xii. Tests

Everything that checks the plugin runs with one command, `scripts/test.sh`: every headless suite in `tests/` (one Neovim per suite, a temporary court and
hollow so nothing of yours is touched), the parse of this README and of the roadmap, the screen checks (`tests/screen.sh`, a real Neovim in tmux, reading what is
drawn) and the corpus of the grammar. It prints one line per part and exits with a failure if any failed.

  -  `scripts/test.sh --fast` skips the screen checks and the corpus (a few seconds): what the pre-commit hook runs; `scripts/test.sh agenda export` runs only those suites
  -  set `FEY_PARSER` to the built `fey.so` (the tests say so if it is missing); the corpus needs the `tree-sitter` command and `FEY_GRAMMAR`, the directory of tree-sitter-fey
  -  `scripts/install_hooks.sh` installs the hook: staged Lua files have to be formatted (`stylua`; it tells you, it does not touch your files) and the fast tests have to pass
  -  `tests/modules.lua` loads every module of the plugin, so a require of a module that is gone fails at once; `tests/tags_matrix.lua` takes every tag in every form through
     parse, index, find at its position, handler, export and completion, and fails when a tag of the table of tags in the roadmap has no sample: a new tag is one line of its `SAMPLES`
  -  a suite is a Lua file that prints `name: N checks, M failures` and quits with a failure when M is not 0; copy the temporary vault of `tests/api.lua` or
     `tests/links.lua` for a new one. A change of the grammar needs a corpus case in `test/corpus` of tree-sitter-fey (`tree-sitter test -u` writes the expected tree; read it)


  II.M.xiii. Words

The plugin began as orgmode for Neovim and some of its words mean something else in Fey. Say these: the words of a heading are its **labels**
(a tag is only the syntax, the brackets and the name), a date tag holds a **date** and not a timestamp, the planning lines are **planning tags**, a drawer is a pair or block tag,
a directive is a **key of the document data**, an agenda file is the **vault**, a src block is a fenced block. The glossary is in `legal/contributing.fey`.

  -  renamed: the options `fey_use_label_inheritance`, `fey_labels_exclude_from_inheritance`, `fey_agenda_remove_labels` and `fey_date_rounding_minutes`; the mappings
     `fey_set_labels_command`, `fey_agenda_set_labels`, `fey_date_up`, `fey_date_down`, `fey_date_up_day`, `fey_date_down_day`, `fey_date_insert`, `fey_date_insert_inactive`
     and `fey_toggle_date_type`; in the Lua API `get_labels`, `set_labels`, `add_label`, `has_label`, `labels_to_string`, `get_planning_dates`, `get_heading_path`, `get_data_key`, `is_src`
  -  the old names keep working for a release: `setup` moves the old options and mappings to the new names and says so once, a note's `plugin` tag may use the old option names,
     `:checkhealth fey` lists the old names the setup used, and the old methods call the new ones and say so once (`vim.deprecate`)
  -  `scripts/terms.lua` lists where the old words are (`nvim --headless --clean -l scripts/terms.lua`); `--check` fails on a new use of one, and runs with the tests


  II.M.xiv. The documentation

The documentation is Fey files, in `docs/`: the index, installation, a tutorial that follows the real features, configuration, the reference of the tags, the mappings, plugins and tools, troubleshooting,
contributing (with the glossary) and the changelog. `:Fey help` opens them in Neovim, as a hollow of their own, so the links between the pages are checked by the index like any other links.

  -  the references cannot drift, they are made from the code: every option with its type, default, description, whether a `plugin` tag may set it and whether the change takes effect at once (the white list
     `fey.settings.fey_options` decides), the options of the editor a `nvim` tag may not set, every tag with its forms, head, body, what the index keeps and what open at point does
     (`lua/fey/docs/tags.lua`, which `tests/docs.lua` keeps complete: a `*_tag_name` option with no entry fails), the mappings, and the changelog from the history
  -  `scripts/gen_docs.lua` writes the generated parts of the pages (between the comment tags `begin generated` and `end generated`), `--check` fails when one is out of date
  -  `scripts/build_docs.sh` makes the help file `doc/fey.txt` and its tags: the pages are exported to Markdown by the plugin, pandoc reads that and `scripts/panvimdoc.lua` writes the help; the pre-commit hook does it when
     the pages or what they are made from are staged. `doc/fey_api.txt` is written by hand and `tests/docs.lua` checks that every public function of the API is in it
  -  `tests/docs.lua` also checks that every page parses, that every link of the pages resolves in a vault of a copy of the docs, and that the help file and its tags have every page


  II.M.xv. Import, and the command line

Markdown (Obsidian's flavour too) and org come in as Fey. The importers do not read the text themselves: they walk the tree of the tree-sitter parsers, `markdown` and `markdown_inline`
(nvim-treesitter installs them) and `org` (the one the orgmode plugin installs; `FEY_ORG_PARSER` names the file, and `lazy/*/parser/org.so` and `site/parser/org.so` under the data directory are looked
in). What the grammars do not know is matched in the text between the nodes: the emphasis markers of org, and Obsidian's own syntax.

  -  `:FeyImport [markdown|org] [rename]` changes the buffer in place, in one undo step (the format comes from the name or the filetype); `rename` also gives the buffer the `.fey` name. `:FeyExport [format]` is the
     export of `<prefix>e`: a menu, or the format given; the note stays and the result is a new file next to it
  -  `require('fey.import')`: `.text(src, format, opts)` gives the Fey text and the notes about what did not come across, `.buffer(bufnr, format, opts)`, `.file(path, opts)` and `.files(paths, opts)`; `opts.write` is `adjacent` (the
     default: `name.fey` next to the source, which is kept), `replace` (the Fey text over the file itself) or `rename` (over the file, with the extension `.fey`); `force` writes over a file that exists, `dry_run` writes nothing,
     `link_extension = false` leaves the `.md` and `.org` targets of links alone
  -  `bin/fey` on the command line, for batches (it runs a headless Neovim, `NVIM_BIN` names it, `FEY_PARSER` the Fey parser when Neovim does not find it):

         ###  src sh
         bin/fey import notes/*.md                       # a .fey next to each note
         bin/fey import --write replace --format org *.txt
         bin/fey import --write rename --dry-run vault/**/*.org
         bin/fey export --format html --outdir out docs/*.fey
         bin/fey export --format pdf notes/plan.fey
         ###

     an export is always a new file (`--force` to write over one), the Fey file is kept; a file that fails does not stop the others, the exit status is 1 if any did, and the notes about what was dropped go to the
     standard error
  -  Markdown comes in as: front matter keys as the `table` data tag of the document (`tags` as the labels of the document, lists joined), nested maps and lists as a block `table` tag with a keyed bullet for each key, headings by their level (the Fey signatures are made, a skipped level
     is not), emphasis, strike and code, links (a `.md` target becomes `.fey`), `[[wikilinks]]` with their alias, `![[embeds]]` and images as a `link` with `embed: true`, `#tags` as `labels` tags,
     task boxes with Obsidian's other marks (`[/]`, `[!]`, ...), the dates of the Tasks plugin, callouts and quotes as a `blockquote` with a class, tables (the header gets the rule under it), fenced
     blocks, `$math$` and `$$display$$`, footnotes (references and definitions), `%%comments%%` and HTML comments as `comment` tags. HTML blocks are kept as `html` source blocks. Inline HTML elements are pair tags with the element's name and its attributes as keys (`[ span; class: big #]words[# span ]`), a tag with no partner is an `html` line tag with the tag as its text. With `root` (the directory of the vault) and `db_dir` (its `.fey/dbs`; `--root` and `--db-dir` on the command line) the Dataview ```` ```dataview ```` blocks become `query` block tags (the folders of `FROM` are looked up in the vault, `arch-ive-path` is `arch_ive_path`) and Database Folder views (```` ```yaml:dbfolder ````) become a database file with the columns, the source folder and the filters, and a `feydb` tag in the note. A Dataview inline field alone on a line before the first heading (`parent:: [[note]]`) is a key of the table data of the document (a link is its target)
  -  org comes in as: `#+title:` and the other keywords as document data (`#+filetags:` as labels, `#+todo:` as the keywords), headings with their keyword and priority as the `status` tag and their
     tags as labels, `SCHEDULED:`, `DEADLINE:` and `CLOSED:` as planning tags, the property drawer as a `prop` tag, `CLOCK:` lines as `clock` tags in a `logbook`, other drawers as block tags, lists with boxes
     (`[-]` is `[/]`), tables (a `#+tblfm:` line becomes a `tblfm` line tag under the table, the formula as its body), source blocks with their arguments and `#+name:`, quote blocks, footnotes, timestamps in the text as `date` tags, and links (`file:` and `.org` targets become `.fey`)
  -  not carried: highlights (`==x==`), links to a heading of the same file, blocks of other kinds in org (kept as comments), and tag openers in
     the text, which cannot be written in a paragraph (a blank is put inside). Each of these is named in the notes the import gives back. The result is read back with the Fey grammar and a syntax error is one of the notes
  -  adjacent lists of different kinds (a bulleted and a numbered one) are one list in Fey



  II.N. Queries and databases

Notes can hold queries (a Dataview style language, with TABLE, LIST and TASK) and
database views (Obsidian Bases style) that are computed from the vault. The
tutorial in the test vault, `README.fey` in the vault directory, is both the
manual and the fixture for them. Their mappings are `<prefix>qq` and `<prefix>qa`
to run queries, `<prefix>bn` `<prefix>bs` `<prefix>bv` `<prefix>bt` and
`<prefix>bl` for databases.


  II.O. Currently Available Default Mappings

This list is made from `lua/fey/config/defaults.lua` by `scripts/gen_mappings.lua`,
so it is always what is actually mapped. Mappings that are commented out or empty
there do not appear.

-- begin generated: mappings (scripts/gen_mappings.lua) --

`<prefix>` is `<Leader>;` unless `mappings.prefix` says otherwise. A mapping is changed or turned off in
the setup, under the name in the last column: `mappings = { fey = { fey_refile = "<prefix>R" } }`, or
`false` to turn it off.

Anywhere:

| keys       | does                                                                   | name                |
+============+========================================================================+=====================+
| <prefix>a  | Open the agenda prompt                                                 | fey_agenda          |
| <prefix>c  | Capture a new note or task                                             | fey_capture         |
| <prefix>bn | Open a new database view of all files in the vault                     | fey_db_new          |
| <prefix>bc | Open a new database view in the current window, full screen            | fey_db_new_current  |
| <prefix>bs | Open a new database view in a horizontal split                         | fey_db_new_split    |
| <prefix>bt | Open a new database view in a new tab                                  | fey_db_new_tab      |
| <prefix>bv | Open a new database view in a vertical split                           | fey_db_new_vsplit   |
| <prefix>bl | Pick an existing database to open                                      | fey_db_pick         |
| <prefix>bL | Pick an existing database and open it in the current window            | fey_db_pick_current |
| <prefix>vi | Make the cwd a hollow (create its .fey folder) and index its Fey files | fey_hollow_init     |
| <prefix>vj | Browse the hollows from the court down and jump to one                 | fey_hollow_jump     |
| <prefix>vJ | Browse the hollows and open the one you pick in a new tab              | fey_hollow_jump_tab |
| <prefix>vr | Update the vault index                                                 | fey_vault_reindex   |

